// What two parties are to each other, written once.
//
// A kind of contract declares its roles (`has employee person`) and the legs that follow from it, with the roles for ends. A
// contract of the kind fills the roles (`employee me`), and each book that owns a contract of it has the legs it touches:
//
//   * a role its owner (or a member of the owner) fills stands at the account the schedule pays from or into;
//   * a role anyone else fills stands outside, at the party;
//   * a leg with both ends outside moves value between two parties, which no book of an owner of neither has anything to say
//     of, and is not made.
//
// So the employer's half of the payroll tax below is in the employer's book and not in the household's, from one line. The
// copies of 05-family and 07-landlord in this folder write their contracts by it and check to what the originals check to
// (`verify.sh`); `employer/` is the book it is true in from the other side.

/// An employment: one pays the other for work. The employer pays the other half of the payroll tax, which is the same 7.65% of
/// the gross (6.2% social security, 1.45% medicare) whoever's book it is in, and the employee's is withheld from the pay.
kind employment : contract
  has employee person
  has employer employer
  also employer -> irs 7.65% of amount #payroll-tax

/// A lease: a tenant lives in what a landlord owns. Nothing follows from a lease that is not a line of the contract (the rent,
/// the deposit, the dates), so a lease is its two roles for now.
kind lease : contract
  has landlord landlord
  has tenant tenant

/// A management agreement: a manager keeps a property for its client (who owns it), for a fee that the contract says.
kind management : contract
  has client person
  has manager org
